guff's native goimports gets import-group blank lines wrong in two independent
ways, both only when the source is not already correctly grouped. Neither needs
`local-prefixes`: everything below reproduces with a plain
`formatters: {enable: [goimports]}`.

## A. No blank is added when the specs are already sorted

    import (            upstream            guff
        "fmt"           "fmt"               "fmt"
        "os"            "os"                "os"
        "github.com/x/y"                    "github.com/x/y"
    )                   <blank>
                        "github.com/x/y"

Localized. `crates/guff-fmt/src/native/goimports/mod.rs` short-circuits with

    if fixes.is_empty()
        && !import_runs_need_sort(&imps, &local)
        && !needs_import_decl_merge(&file)

and `import_runs_need_sort` only asks whether each run is out of *sort* order.
Upstream goimports always runs `addImportSpaces`, so a run that is correctly
sorted but missing a group separator still gets one — which this early exit
skips. The missing third predicate is "a group boundary inside a run has no
blank line in the source" (and its converse).

## B. A blank appears between two imports of the *same* group

Minimal case — three standard-library imports, one block, unsorted:

    import (
        "fmt"
        "os"
        "bytes"
    )

upstream writes `bytes`, `fmt`, `os` with no blank anywhere. guff writes
`bytes`, `fmt`, <blank>, `os`.

All three are `import_group` 0, so **the group logic cannot be the cause** —
which rules out the obvious suspect, and the one the emit loop is built around.
It is also not simply "any reorder": two imports that need swapping come out
right, in the same group or across groups. It takes three, in one block, with
the sort actually moving something.

Not localized. Two further guesses were checked and both were wrong; they are
recorded so the next reader does not repeat them:

  * `detect_runs` keys on original source line numbers, but `arrange` only
    applies add/delete/rename fixes — it does not reorder — and `reconstruct`
    sorts *within* each run, so the run indices stay valid.
  * The within-run separator fires only on an `import_group` change, and case B
    has no group change at all.

Reading was not enough. The next attempt should instrument `reconstruct` — in
particular what `imp_bytes` returns for each spec, since a spec whose recorded
source range carries its own newline would produce exactly this, and would
explain why it depends on which spec moves where.

## Why no existing gate saw either

`regress/fmt_diff.py --formatter goimports` compares against the real
`goimports` over corpora that are all already correctly grouped, so neither
path is ever entered. Same shape as docs/COMPAT-HARDENING.md 続き 87, where a
gofmt-clean corpus hid the `go/doc/comment` round trip.

Both A and B were checked against the real `goimports` binary as well as
`golangci-lint fmt`, so this is guff's defect and not a wrapper difference.
